#!/bin/sh
# Start or continue a run against a named open conjecture.
#
# The Euler wrapper fetches a statement and hands over a task that fits on a
# page, because a Project Euler problem has one number as its answer and a
# ceiling on how long it can reasonably take. An open conjecture has neither.
# The task here is phased instead: build the library, work the loop, and never
# claim the conjecture. The phases overlap on purpose. A first version made
# phase 1 sequential and its exit condition "the literature is exhausted", which
# is not checkable: a live run spent twenty-seven uninterrupted minutes there,
# reached fifty-three sources and a rich ROOT.md, and never wrote a single
# belief into the brief every role reads — it had read everything and
# concluded nothing.
set -eu

repository_root=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." && pwd)
cd "$repository_root"

usage() {
    echo "usage: ./conjecture [--no-research] <slug> [extra instructions...]" >&2
    echo "" >&2
    echo "  <slug>  a folder under workspace/conjectures/ holding problem.md and GOAL.md" >&2
    echo "  --no-research  withhold web search; almost never right for an open problem" >&2
    echo "  --schools a,b  run these schools concurrently (chisel, rising-sea," >&2
    echo "                 adversarial); default is chisel alone" >&2
}

research=on
schools=${MATH_AGENT_SCHOOLS:-}
while [ "$#" -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
        --no-research) research=off; shift ;;
        --research) research=on; shift ;;
        --schools)
            if [ "$#" -lt 2 ]; then
                usage
                exit 2
            fi
            schools=$2
            shift 2
            ;;
        -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
        *) break ;;
    esac
done

if [ "$#" -lt 1 ]; then
    usage
    exit 2
fi

slug=$1
shift
case "$slug" in
    ""|*/*|.*)
        echo "slug must be a plain folder name under workspace/conjectures/" >&2
        exit 2
        ;;
esac

# A conjecture takes the same wall clock as anything else, and the twelve hours
# that used to be set here is the reason to say so explicitly.
#
# That override was written to protect the attempt: the wall clock raises a hard
# Timeout, so reaching it loses the `goals` run's own context and report, and
# twelve hours put it out of reach. `RunBudget` has since made the opposite
# argument, and it is the better one — the attempt only becomes judgeable *by*
# returning, and on an open conjecture, where `goals` is told to pursue the goal
# until it is met, it never returns on its own. The cap is what ends it.
#
# The two are not a matter of taste, because a live run settled it. On one
# evening 241 and 761 were both killed at exactly 30:00, and both had a judge
# verdict, a reflection and an inventor spawn within ten minutes. Erdős–Gyárfás,
# carrying this override, ran fifty-six minutes inside attempt 1 with zero
# verdicts, zero reflections and zero inventor spawns — the precise failure the
# thirty-minute default was written to stop, on the one run type its reasoning
# names. What is lost at the cap is the run's context and its report; what is on
# disk survives, and `continuation_briefing` is what lets the next attempt pick
# it up. That is a far smaller loss than never being judged at all.
#
# Set `MATH_AGENT_RUN_MINUTES` to override for a specific investigation.

workspace_subdir=conjectures/$slug
workspace_path=$repository_root/workspace/$workspace_subdir
if [ ! -f "$workspace_path/problem.md" ]; then
    echo "no statement at workspace/$workspace_subdir/problem.md" >&2
    exit 2
fi

# The method belongs to the workspace, not to this script.
#
# This block read "Solve by structural graph theory ... connectivity, girth,
# degree distribution, forbidden subgraphs, separators, ear decompositions, DFS
# trees and their back edges, expansion", and the oracle phase asked for a
# checker "given a graph, its minimum degree and the set of its cycle lengths".
# That is Erdős–Gyárfás, and every conjecture launched afterwards was told to do
# it. A live magic-square-of-squares run — a question about rational points on
# an elliptic curve, with no graph anywhere in it — spent eighteen minutes being
# told to hunt a minimal counterexample by girth and to encode degree bounds for
# a SAT solver. It worked despite the instruction rather than because of it,
# because `problem.md` and `GOAL.md` said what the problem actually was.
#
# So the domain-specific half now comes from `METHOD.md` beside the statement.
# A workspace that does not supply one gets a neutral instruction, which is
# worse than a method written for the problem and far better than a method
# written for a different problem.
method_path=$workspace_path/METHOD.md
if [ -f "$method_path" ]; then
    method=$(cat "$method_path")
else
    method="Choose the line of attack the problem calls for and say which one you have chosen and why. Reason about the structure a counterexample or a solution must have, and use every other capability in service of that argument rather than instead of it."
fi

extra_instructions=$*
task="Attack the open conjecture stated in /workspace/problem.md. Your objective and the criteria that end this run are in /workspace/GOAL.md. Read both before doing anything else.

This is an open problem. It has resisted specialists for decades, so the working assumption is that you will not prove it, and the deliverable is a genuine partial result stated exactly rather than a claim of the whole. Claiming the conjecture on an argument that has not survived attack is the one outright failure available here.

$method

Work in these phases. They overlap: 1 and 2 run together, and 3 starts as soon as 2 has a claim worth testing. A phase you cannot exit is a phase that consumes the run.

1. BUILD THE LIBRARY, AND STOP WHEN IT IS ENOUGH. Spawn librarian and research immediately. This phase is finished the moment research/ROOT.md states the structure of a minimal counterexample, the current verification bound, and at least three restricted classes already settled with their hypotheses — not when the literature is exhausted, which is a condition nobody can check and which will hold your run in this phase forever. Have scholar digest each source into claim blocks as it lands. Follow research/FRONTIER.md rather than inventing queries. When ROOT.md meets that test, say so and move on; further gathering from then on happens only against a stated gap in research/REQUESTS.md.

2. EXTRACT AS YOU GO, NOT AFTERWARDS. Do not wait for phase 1 to finish. Every time a source lands and the scholar says what it establishes, record it where the run will actually read it: a fenced claim block in the note, so it reaches research/CLAIMS.md with its hypotheses, its holds-here and its status; and a line under Established in CONTEXT.md, which is the brief every role is sent on every model call. Each belief states its evidence class (proved / verified-numerically / conjectured / asserted-by-source) and what would falsify it — a belief with no falsifier cannot be attacked, and attacking it is the next step. Record every closed direction under Ruled out with the obstruction that closed it. You are behind if research/ has content and CONTEXT.md's Established section is empty — that means the run has read things and believes nothing, which is the failure this phase exists to prevent. State plainly which obstruction defeated each previous attempt, because your approach has to beat that same obstruction.

3. BUILD THE ORACLE. Have tool_builder write an exact, obviously-correct checker for the objects this problem is about; GOAL.md says what it has to decide and what it is measured against. Verify it against small cases by hand. Then use it to reproduce the literature's verification bound before trusting anything you compute past it. A computational claim built on an unchecked checker is worth nothing.

4. THEN LOOP. Each attempt states one precise structural claim about a minimal counterexample, attacks it before trusting it — hunt the counterexample as seriously as the proof — and either establishes it, refutes it, or leaves it open with the gap named exactly. Use sat_solver for the finite questions the argument throws off: whether an object meeting a finite list of stated constraints exists at all is a satisfiability question, and UNSAT on it is a theorem. Use lean_prover to formalise the statement early and each lemma as it stabilises; report #print axioms output and every remaining sorry. Use coder for anything the run's conclusions rest on.

Delegate: tool_builder and coder run programs, sat_solver encodes a finite question for CP-SAT or a SAT solver, smt_solver settles one over a theory with Z3 and cvc5 and can prove a universal claim by refuting its negation, theorem_prover hands first-order statements to eprover, symbolic_math derives and checks closed forms with sympy/PARI/Singular, lean_prover writes Lean 4 against a pre-built Mathlib, research and librarian and scholar own the library, pattern_finder reads the numbers you compute, inventor proposes a different line when one stalls. Spawn asynchronously, keep every run ID, await every result you rely on.

Never state a theorem, citation, bound, or computation result you did not obtain. Distinguish a proof from a numerical check from a heuristic from a sourced claim, every time, in every file you write."

if [ -n "$extra_instructions" ]; then
    task="$task

Additional instructions: $extra_instructions"
fi

exec env MATH_AGENT_RESEARCH="$research" MATH_AGENT_SCHOOLS="$schools" \
    ./agent --workspace "$workspace_subdir" "$task"
